ain. They replaced many of these roles through "cadre deployment" (prioritizing party loyalty over competence). This was a conscious, strategic choice made by the democratic government, not an inevitable force of nature caused by apartheid. **Conclusion** You are absolutely right that apartheid set the board, stacked the deck, and handed the new government a deeply broken system. The historical trauma and structural deficits cannot be overstated. But history only dictates the starting line, not every step of the race. At a certain point, the people running the system must take ownership of how they are running it. Apartheid explains the fragility of the South African state, but the greed, deliberate looting, and ethical failures of modern leaders are choices they made all on their own.
